 of like you're computing  the net force  so basically uh when we regularize  sparsity in this message domain  we're basically like encouraging it to  find the force vector  because that's that's a low dimensional  representation  and then your node model might end up  being the acceleration  so you would you would approximate both  the edge model the known model  and convert those uh  with symbolic regression into an  equation  okay so so we tried this for many  different uh  data sets so we have like one over r  squared one over r  spring law we even have an if statement  describing a force law  so this is a really nasty kind of  interaction law  and so we can get we can actually  recover those expressions  so we start by generating these  simulations of maybe particles connected  by springs  so these are different simulations  recorded over time  and this is the true force law so we  learn to predict these dynamics  with a graph network so you observe and  you can see  over time they get closer and closer to  the true dynamics  just from the graph network learning  this regression problem now what we also  do is we regularize the internal  functions of the graph network  to have small dimensional latent spaces  so you see it kind of pushes this  so that it puts as much information to  as few dimensions as possible  um so it sparsifies the light in space  so one thing you can do is just fit the  forces to those messages  and it turns out that it's actually  those  messages are actually a rotation of the  true forces  so you can see it's it's almost a  perfect correlation  when you have this rotation so if if  this law is unknown  rather than fitting the force you would  apply symbolic regression  so you would kind of fit these messages  and you can see you extract this  expression it's the rotated spring law  which is pretty cool so we can we can do  this for many different systems  we can recover spring law one over r  squared  we can even recover like nasty  discontinuous forces  we also did this with uh hamiltonian  neural networks  so we have this like hamiltonian graph  network and we can recover  the interaction hamiltonian between two  particles  the actual analytic expression which is  pretty cool um  so we also so being an astronomer i  i kind of interested in this technique  to to apply it to astrophysical systems  so we have this this dark matter  simulation this  very kind of uh this very large  dark matter simulation over many  different cosmologies  and we try to basically predict  the dark matter over density at the  center of  a dark matter halo and so we can  convert the graph network into an  analytic expression we get this  this cool kind of over density  expression  and it turns out it's more accurate than  than um  kind of the the most intuitive  expression you could write down  so you can see that kind of  counter-intuitively you're dividing by  mass where you would expect to multiply  by mass  so you can actually you can start to  kind of  think about how you would theoretically  get this result which is pretty cool and  you you  you really can't have that discussion  with a normal neural network  you can only really discuss these kind  of these scaling relations  when you do symbolic regression on your  graph network so it's  very interpretable in terms of existing  physics  you get good generalization which we  observed um  it's it's of course accurate it's  compact it's a really nice expression so  we did this  we tested this kind of like wignerian  prior idea so when you convert it into  an  expression even though it's not the true  expression necessarily  because it's like this this dark matter  relation you would not expect this to be  a true relationship because it's kind of  just it's kind of just a correlation  but when you actually measure the  generalization to out-of-distribution  data  against the graph network it destroys  the graph network  like this analytic expression is much  better at generalizing  out of distribution data and i think  that's something that's really  interesting  as we go forward in developing machine  learning for physics  so restatement you make a neural network  you try to have multiple internal  functions  that each work on a low dimensional  space  you then approximate that  function with a symbolic equation so you  can use  like cindy for if you know your basis  functions piscer if you don't um and  then you try to compose those symbolic  expressions together  and that's kind of an equivalent  interpretable model so  so one thing some people ask us is why  don't you do straight symbolic  regression you have this  you have the sum pool um maybe you can  learn  this expression in this expression  simultaneously the the problem with this  is that  you square the number of possible  equations it's already a hard problem to  do this with symbolic regression  for just just one of these equations but  now you're kind of  squaring the number because you're  fitting them simultaneously whereas with  the neural network  you can fit z and y i simultaneously  very fast just  by a chain rule so you only get two to  the nine  two times ten to the nine possibilities  because you fit them separately  um so it's it scales much better  um so uh we we  developed this technique last year we've  been applying it to different  um problems in astrophysics so one is  like finding  optimal observables for cosmic voids you  can actually extract  a analytic expression for the  property of a void which is kind of most  uh  which provides the most information for  predicting  uh cosmological properties so this is  with christina  pablo and niall led this really cool  work um  where they they find newton's law from  real solar system data and they also  learn  the planetary masses from scratch so we  have to kind of give  the masses to the graph network but they  actually can  learn the masses which is really cool  so the another application is we're  trying to apply this graph network  approach to fluid dynamics  where you basically train the graph  network on some turbulence problem  and then you try to extract the pdes  from the graph network  so obviously like these are all  expressions from the same equation  um this is a lot more difficult so we've  we've been developed some  techniques to get this working this is  with elaine  this is also kind of a really cool  application of this idea  so again the takeaways are symbolic  regression machine learning algorithm  where you can  you can model your data with analytic  expressions so it's much  more interpretable it might provide  better generalization for physics  you can always kind of convert a deep  neural network into an analytic  expression with this  technique i described and i think this  is a very strong way of interpretation  this also represents a good way of doing  symbolic correction on high dimensional  data whereas normal symbolic regression  kind of  uh it scales scales badly  um so our code we have a tutorial  online uh piscer this symbolic  regression package i used  it's on github as well yeah thanks for  listening  i'm interested to see kind of what  applications people do with this  technique thank you
 

